#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161645 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/id #uuid "1fdcc887-4c52-5d91-ab28-63a7dce9fe5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12462" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\n- $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = f(0,0) = 0.28.$ Likewise, \n\n$$\nP(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0) = f_X(0) \\cdot f_Y(0) = 0.4 \\cdot 0.7 = 0.28. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =0.12=0.4\\cdot 0.3={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.42=0.6\\cdot 0.7={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.18=0.6\\cdot 0.3={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/id #uuid "4e12d6b6-5771-5e54-8f16-7899f82d90bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12463" :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(4,2).$\n\n| | $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | |  | | $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.55$ | $0.45$ | |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ |  |  |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.55$ | $0.45$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161647 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/id #uuid "1875d098-95f6-50a7-b069-56865207b3dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12464" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.24$ | $0.06$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.56$ | $0.14$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | |  | | $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.8$ | $0.2$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.8$ | $0.2$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\n- $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = f(0,0) = 0.24.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0) = f_X(0) \\cdot f_Y(0) = 0.3 \\cdot 0.8 = 0.24. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =0.56=0.7\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(0,1) & =0.06=0.3\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.14=0.7\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a30d7ab4-10f5-5989-8ec2-ec2450038780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158959} {:answer/id #uuid "a68b8f71-35a7-5bd6-88c9-f76507cf3663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158960} {:answer/id #uuid "d838d916-561d-559f-ab56-d23ff5056d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158961} {:answer/id #uuid "078da2a7-4e8e-57e1-828d-d09cdc3042e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158962} {:answer/id #uuid "7eadfd30-14b8-531e-b0d7-b14b2ec02b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "078da2a7-4e8e-57e1-828d-d09cdc3042e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158962} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a73765d-3ec2-5ef1-99c0-ea233822ea71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3006b2b-d3f6-5ddf-b811-934181f67f97" :question/math-academy-id "q-110385" :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(3,1).$\n\n| | $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ | |  | | $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.25$ | $0.75$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.25$ | $0.75$ |  |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,1) & ={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) \\\\  & =0.5\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7f01881-8a5d-5aca-aa89-7656519439e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388801} {:answer/id #uuid "496793ad-cb19-5de8-8def-6c425712bc9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388802} {:answer/id #uuid "97e7a0dd-4c48-57b8-b0a8-b7e1d345f336" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388803} {:answer/id #uuid "64082dec-f9fd-5546-982b-b10ee63a022d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388804} {:answer/id #uuid "96b07ab5-1360-56b8-87a6-766301bb826b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7f01881-8a5d-5aca-aa89-7656519439e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388801} :answer-field/id #uuid "37b7e20b-d01b-5ea9-862a-6e152f5a8648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56213840-a75e-5e1c-80a9-c88b8fb4f43b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143317" :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(1,1).$\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | |  | | $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.35$ | |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ |  |  |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.35$ |" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & ={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) \\\\  & =0.4\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06293fc4-9606-5e68-872b-9f1f16ac22cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388808} {:answer/id #uuid "4e57c398-7536-5f02-956f-8819449c0266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388809} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5d965a-1af1-549b-a2f4-e81dd3208989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388810} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f6e4e2-9922-5bd2-88c6-37c6e78e861e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388811} {:answer/id #uuid "95027c1d-90d1-5e28-bd94-e0bb23c15824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06293fc4-9606-5e68-872b-9f1f16ac22cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388808} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4ee55fd-879c-59fd-931d-76c0cf18cba1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36c0f5bb-c570-5fab-9e2c-17aeec511666" :question/math-academy-id "q-143331" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=2,Y=1)=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=2,Y=1)=f(2,1)=0.15.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.2\\cdot 0.75=0.15.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.6=0.8\\cdot 0.75={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,2) & =0.2=0.8\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,2) & =0.05=0.2\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6307f39-aa75-5ac5-8dcf-5fd8dd59e722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388815} {:answer/id #uuid "aacc8291-9b34-59eb-9b28-7e83b27c735e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388816} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cd5621-e4ed-568c-afd2-d331b4e9bedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388817} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c8ec7-141d-566d-8f93-8f3286bd89dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388818} {:answer/id #uuid "cccf6f68-4a3c-5f9c-9e0b-b4c884e5e844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c8ec7-141d-566d-8f93-8f3286bd89dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388818} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e3f0a9c-f22f-58e2-9d46-8a0e4e7dc989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "663c55cf-94be-539d-a4ab-2ce2f80f8b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143333" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=2,Y=2)=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=2,Y=2)=f(2,2)=0.4.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.5\\cdot 0.6=0.3.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=2,Y=2)\\ne P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=2),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90343c62-8583-5ddb-bf3d-f9389edd437c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388822} {:answer/id #uuid "71b98645-0661-5460-9c0f-3513b5de8375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388823} {:answer/id #uuid "834ca189-7c43-52d7-8c61-ead786e2a56b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388824} {:answer/id #uuid "77da6207-54fc-51bb-88d5-faecf8e8a6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388825} {:answer/id #uuid "180c2a86-2a44-5f58-9f48-98da4c02c597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77da6207-54fc-51bb-88d5-faecf8e8a6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388825} :answer-field/id #uuid "733aaca0-5ece-54bc-bf37-8d32f55e8c22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95d6f6bd-d742-58ed-9bdb-09fd0ac2ef6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143334" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.6$ | $0.15$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=0,Y=3)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=3)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.6$ | $0.15$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=3)=f(0,3)=0.15.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=3)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(3)=0.25\\cdot 0.3=0.075.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=0,Y=3)\\ne P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=3),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c41b292-9e85-5146-807e-a200ba094195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388829} {:answer/id #uuid "f26b8ca0-efed-5318-92df-02c3295ef18e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388830} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3f7880-abf6-5920-b463-c12ef810730b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388831} {:answer/id #uuid "28e88ab9-0ac6-5e73-8ad5-af7bcd6b31de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388832} {:answer/id #uuid "56f2c3bb-40a6-5f82-9f21-39c62100a6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c3f7880-abf6-5920-b463-c12ef810730b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388831} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8c3df04-f116-513b-9b27-a23a2f809841" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ae8181a-5342-50eb-83a8-1b8154c2d606" :question/math-academy-id "q-143338" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.16$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.64$ | $0.09$ | $0.07$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=3,Y=2)=P(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.16$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.64$ | $0.09$ | $0.07$ | $0.8$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.8$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=3,Y=2)=f(3,2)=0.64.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.8\\cdot 0.8=0.64.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =0.16=0.2\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,4) & =0.01\\ne 0.2\\cdot 0.1={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(4) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27603902-3215-56d0-9ac7-9b672afdb44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388836} {:answer/id #uuid "84f089e8-65e7-5b00-a051-87a31b061623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388837} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2ce828-cd82-58f1-a10e-677ec80cf40f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388838} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3c34b1-bb1d-5c52-b288-c4aa15dc5e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388839} {:answer/id #uuid "473b6a86-0787-532f-aaa5-d7aca06b5369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d3c34b1-bb1d-5c52-b288-c4aa15dc5e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388839} :answer-field/id #uuid "16252275-4dba-5e15-bbbe-7de03faa9f2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67169354-a69e-5f24-91da-a2d34af67099" :question/math-academy-id "q-143435" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ | $0.7$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.3$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.35$ | $0.65$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.05.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.3\\cdot 0.65=0.195.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=1)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5c27e92-c78e-5458-87f4-43ba0f932f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388843} {:answer/id #uuid "985c4365-c0d4-5fa0-a55e-c41f1c0be7b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388844} {:answer/id #uuid "74109c17-3de0-5383-a632-1aca9994842e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388845} {:answer/id #uuid "94026d59-3505-5d93-9b18-c94e762b7e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388846} {:answer/id #uuid "85edc7a8-b7a4-5f74-9c49-a752fa07ab43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94026d59-3505-5d93-9b18-c94e762b7e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388846} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4e51138-4831-5e32-b9c2-6e2ce3977be0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "041fb261-106c-5612-9203-07ac7e54c65c" :question/math-academy-id "q-149651" :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(4,1).$\n\n| | $x$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | |  | | $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ |  |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ |  |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4,1) & ={f}_{X}(4)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db34835-16ee-5414-9ba9-90dec7000a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159841} {:answer/id #uuid "9f70be31-7e9c-5328-88b9-7fda1a5ee688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159842} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e6ed0c-1ea1-5637-8d7c-babad863c1d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159843} {:answer/id #uuid "e204d1f8-3ac8-59b0-93f8-32c3cf3a0c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159844} {:answer/id #uuid "4995cc44-2fa4-50ef-9a88-23a2e6d2a5b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8db34835-16ee-5414-9ba9-90dec7000a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159841} :answer-field/id #uuid "5780cbb8-161c-53f4-af21-d1a568c66f19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b10e877-0140-5f79-97b2-25494c500279" :question/math-academy-id "q-162744" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ | |  | | $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.7\\cdot 0.8=0.56.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=1)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed36b314-b38c-58d8-abca-993e0298eb66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388850} {:answer/id #uuid "70c23ca5-fbf0-5e26-bac8-fbadf8baebbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388851} {:answer/id #uuid "b2eea6f0-c237-5c22-b606-c0aabee4105a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388852} {:answer/id #uuid "f3cd3243-fdfb-5baf-9d9d-df498ee394b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388853} {:answer/id #uuid "a77b268c-dcf5-5f2d-8284-b9153954e4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70c23ca5-fbf0-5e26-bac8-fbadf8baebbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388851} :answer-field/id #uuid "10d8d4c3-b2f2-53de-8759-012443bd2779" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db7ff2bd-ad8f-5f53-98fe-f7bf18596cef" :question/math-academy-id "q-162746" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.06$ | $0.04$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.5$ | $0.24$ | $0.06$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | |  | | $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |  |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=1)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.06.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.2\\cdot 0.3=0.06.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =0.1\\ne 0.2\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f403fd01-3620-505b-b5c4-db964c81d9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388857} {:answer/id #uuid "92a17821-c36a-5b8d-9aa6-f4515f21dff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388858} {:answer/id #uuid "28b1398b-9d65-5e24-aef4-03a34162dc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388859} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3bff92-d7e6-5a44-ae56-e98325dbf010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388860} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb06ec0-f69d-565a-b901-460d6ade6d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f403fd01-3620-505b-b5c4-db964c81d9f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388857} :answer-field/id #uuid "bec03a4a-4d14-5cec-9215-cbaf2fa8f03f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61217abd-1199-5f0c-bc56-23b219ed6d83" :question/math-academy-id "q-162824" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | |  | | $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=1,Y=0)=P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=0)=f(1,0)=0.25.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0)=0.5\\cdot 0.6=0.3.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=1,Y=0)\\ne P(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=0),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "386ab619-11e7-5250-82d0-3f61753dfed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388864} {:answer/id #uuid "8471a0da-38ed-533c-a0ee-dfea941591d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388865} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad30b59-400a-54a6-b597-1ecd5088d3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388866} {:answer/id #uuid "07589031-8aa9-52a6-a219-8e709b861ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388867} {:answer/id #uuid "e1918e45-b4b0-5463-ac9f-bbdf240c502e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "386ab619-11e7-5250-82d0-3f61753dfed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388864} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b823c6-b423-5ba0-ab6b-7fbdb48acfb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "76a9bd4c-2981-511b-8af4-34bbe22f3289" :question/math-academy-id "q-162899" :question/problem "The partially complete joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $∗$ | $0.35$ | $∗$ |\n| $X=2$ | $∗$ | $0.25$ | $?$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $P(X=2),$ rounded to two decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our random variables are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) \\\\ P(X=2,Y=1) & =P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1) \\\\ 0.25 & =P(X=2)\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ P(X=2) & =\\frac{0.25}{0.6} \\\\  & ≈0.42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b765eb91-db19-5153-bfc2-2e45bcfeef19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388871} {:answer/id #uuid "db97b7d2-8393-525a-942e-e16082a21dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388872} {:answer/id #uuid "f30f4bfa-3b43-56e1-adc1-214e12dbd348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388873} {:answer/id #uuid "88d9904c-d607-553b-bef2-5713e95b316d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388874} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb942e6-aced-5725-82f8-561f2901283e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db97b7d2-8393-525a-942e-e16082a21dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388872} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7e917cc-3927-5b33-bd15-4f4ae618e820" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38e07bbf-77f3-55a5-a231-c35d8518284f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162901" :question/problem "The partially complete joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.03$ | $0.07$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $∗$ | $∗$ | $0.9$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $?$ | $∗$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $P(Y=-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our random variables are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,-1) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-1) \\\\ P(X=2,Y=-1) & =P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=-1) \\\\ 0.03 & =0.1\\cdot P(Y=-1) \\\\ P(Y=-1) & =\\frac{0.03}{0.1} \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df92081-cbd8-550f-a2a5-44e2bf8d816d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388878} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb74ec0-5ae1-528c-a2df-83520cfee88e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388879} {:answer/id #uuid "fd574fbd-ef47-5705-acce-70b1c840546f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388880} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9b0e1c-e4e6-560e-be2c-bf446024134a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388881} {:answer/id #uuid "8a03441b-8699-50ae-a513-730107fa88a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1df92081-cbd8-550f-a2a5-44e2bf8d816d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388878} :answer-field/id #uuid "37369e50-ee96-592c-8beb-e18c62d08f1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54cab6ac-9169-51f6-a0e0-b782401a693c" :question/math-academy-id "q-163605" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{24}$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | |  | | $y$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=0,Y=-2)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=-2)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=-2)=f(0,-2)=\\frac{1}{4}.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=-2)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2)=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =\\frac{5}{12}=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,-2) & =\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{8}={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,2) & =\\frac{5}{24}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b469d35c-6b6a-580d-ae7b-585d23054d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388885} {:answer/id #uuid "73319800-d48a-53c7-8826-f00f47835ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388886} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd761ab-a337-54fd-a5a0-40a936cb7edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388887} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb81eb6-9010-5fb3-9812-8d67dba6312a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388888} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b3efea-7493-5858-a065-fb0d2093db98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73319800-d48a-53c7-8826-f00f47835ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388886} :answer-field/id #uuid "59343d08-c4fa-5b34-b03d-81ac3629c789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e56b75f-6ab0-5af0-91c4-d1bd87640296" :question/math-academy-id "q-163617" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $0.22$ | $0.33$ |\n| $X=8$ | $0.18$ | $0.27$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n| | $x$ | $3$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.55$ | $0.45$ | |  | | $y$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $3$ | $8$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.55$ | $0.45$ |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(X=8,Y=1)=P(X=8)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=8,Y=1)=f(8,1)=0.27.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=8)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(8)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.45\\cdot 0.6=0.27.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,-1) & =0.22=0.55\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-1) & ✓ \\\\ f(3,1) & =0.33=0.55\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(8,-1) & =0.18=0.45\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(8)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
